{{- /* chezmoi:modify-template */ -}}
{{- /*
  ~/.claude/settings.json - the SessionStart matchers of the -axi hooks.

  chezmoi's sourceDir is this directory and its destDir is ~/.claude (setup.sh
  writes both into chezmoi.toml), so this file's target is ~/.claude/settings.json
  and nothing else on the machine is managed. It is a modify_ template because
  settings.json has other writers - Claude Code's /config, plugin installs and
  the -axi installers - so chezmoi owns one field of it, not the whole file.
  The current file arrives on .chezmoi.stdin and the output is the new file.

  Breadcrumb, 2026-09-24T03:00Z. settings.json is JSON and cannot hold a
  comment, so the reasoning for its matchers lives here.

  What broke: the lavish-axi, gh-axi and chrome-devtools-axi SessionStart
  entries in ~/.claude/settings.json each had matcher "" (every source), so they
  also fired on `compact`. Every compaction of every Claude seat, in every
  project, paid about 18 KB of hook output before the rebuilt seat read its own
  checkpoint, most of it lavish-axi's list of 81 sessions - roughly five thousand
  tokens (bearings-v2 runbooks/RB-seat-compaction.md, "Cost to cut"). The lavish
  output also steers a rebuilt Claude seat toward lavish, which the owner's
  global rule forbids for Claude seats outside the firstmate project.

  Why this matcher: the owner approved "startup|resume" for all three on
  2026-09-24 ("Change all three"), over changing only lavish-axi and over leaving
  the file alone. Startup and resume behave as before. skill-sync's own entry
  ("startup|resume|clear", setup.sh HOOK_MATCHER) is a separate decision and this
  template never touches it: an entry is owned only if a hook command in it
  names one of the three tools.

  Why chezmoi: the matcher was first changed by hand with jq at 03:00Z, so
  nothing in git owned it and any of the tools' installers could put "" back
  unnoticed. The owner's rule is no hand edits, and no re-inventing what an
  established tool already does. A hand-rolled jq setter plus a custom drift
  check was written first and rejected: `chezmoi apply` is the setter,
  `chezmoi verify` / `status` / `diff` are the drift check, and both are
  maintained upstream.

  Why the pass-through: toPrettyJson re-serialises with sorted keys, so emitting
  it on every run would reorder the whole file and make `chezmoi verify` fail on
  any key Claude Code appends. The template therefore returns .chezmoi.stdin
  byte for byte unless an owned matcher is actually wrong, and verify fails only
  on real drift. Cost: the one apply that does fix a matcher also sorts the
  file's keys (a cosmetic, one-time rewrite; values and numbers are preserved).

  Cost: after a compaction a seat no longer sees gh-axi's open-PR list or the
  chrome and lavish tool reminders, and runs `gh-axi` itself when it needs them.
  This applies machine wide, the firstmate project included.

  Invalid JSON makes fromJson fail, so `chezmoi apply` exits non-zero and leaves
  the file exactly as it was. A missing or empty file is passed through, so this
  never creates a settings.json.

  Backup of the pre-edit file: ~/.claude/settings.json.20260924T0300Z.bak.
  Record: bearings-v2 memory owner-decisions-2026-09-24, decision 8.
*/ -}}
{{- $want := "startup|resume" -}}
{{- $owned := "lavish-axi|gh-axi|chrome-devtools-axi" -}}
{{- if eq (trim .chezmoi.stdin) "" -}}
{{-   .chezmoi.stdin -}}
{{- else -}}
{{-   $s := fromJson .chezmoi.stdin -}}
{{-   $drift := false -}}
{{-   range $entry := dig "hooks" "SessionStart" list $s -}}
{{-     $mine := false -}}
{{-     range $h := index $entry "hooks" -}}
{{-       if regexMatch $owned (toString (index $h "command")) -}}{{- $mine = true -}}{{- end -}}
{{-     end -}}
{{-     if and $mine (ne (toString (index $entry "matcher")) $want) -}}
{{-       $_ := set $entry "matcher" $want -}}
{{-       $drift = true -}}
{{-     end -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{-   if $drift -}}
{{-     toPrettyJson $s -}}
{{-   else -}}
{{-     .chezmoi.stdin -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{- end -}}
